  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2022 Contribution to Yemen Operational Plan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">An un-earmarked contribution  for the IFRC 2022 Yemen Operational Plan:</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the beginning of the conflict, critical infrastructure was destroyed and over 4 million people fled their homes and remain internally displaced. The conflict led to food insecurity, with more than half of the population facing acute levels of food insecurity and nearing famine. An estimated 24.3 million people in 2020 were need of some form of humanitarian and protection assistance, of whom 14.4 million were in acute need of assistance.
Around 20.5 million Yemenis do not have access to safe water and sanitation, and 20 million cannot access basic healthcare services. Consequently, and over the six years of escalating violent conflict and economic collapse, Yemen has been wrestling with mass outbreaks of preventable diseases, such as cholera, leading to a dramatic deterioration of the humanitarian conditions in the country.
Yemen is also vulnerable and prone to natural hazards. In July 2021, heavy rains and flooding devastated communities in Yemen for the second time this year, causing deaths and injuries, destroying infrastructure and livelihoods, and increasing the spread of deadly diseases</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Yemen</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">current estimate of 24.3 million people in need across the country, noting that the total population of Yemen is estimated at 30.5 million.</narrative>
  </description>
